<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ardashir I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/224">224</a>–242 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ardashir I was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Rising from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, he defeated the last Parthian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artabanus-iv">Artabanus IV</a> in 224 CE and was crowned king of kings. His victory ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a> and established a dynasty that ruled Iran for over four centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ardashir-i.jpg" alt="Ardashir I" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinar_of_Ardashir_I_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early third century CE, while Artabanus IV contended with his brother Vologases VI for the throne, a new power arose in Persis under <b>Ardashir I</b>, a local ruler of the house of Sasan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sasanian Empire was founded in 224 CE when <b>Ardashir I</b>, a local ruler from Pars (modern Fars in south-western Iran) and a vassal of the Arsacid Parthian dynasty, defeated and killed the last Parthian king, Artabanus IV, at the Battle of Hormozdgan, before taking the title Shahanshah,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sasanian dynasty, founded by <b>Ardashir I</b> in 224, made it a state church.</blockquote><a href="/religion/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Other Pahlavi texts fed the poem, chief among them the Kar-Namag i Ardashir i Pabagan, a late Sasanian account of how <b>Ardashir I</b> came to power, with which, according to Zabihollah Safa, various phrases and words of the poem can be matched.</blockquote><a href="/work/shahnameh">Shahnameh · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ardashir I</b>, founder of the Sasanian Empire, had named Shapur his successor in a council of the magnates, and Shapur was crowned co-ruler with him.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-12">12 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At Hormozdgan, <b>Ardashir I</b> defeated the Parthian king Artabanus IV, who was killed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-28">28 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istakhr">Istakhr</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=29.98,52.91&amp;year=233" aria-label="Istakhr on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5925 1069 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ardashir I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6375 1339h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6375 1339h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=29.98,52.91&amp;year=233">What was here in 233 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
